\af0\afs24 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\fs24\insrsid9183818\charrsid9183818 \line Higher discount levels do not appear to improve retention, as all plans have the same 7.98% churn risk (92.02% retention proxy). However, plans with higher discounts gener
ally have lower profit margins. For example, the Basic plan has the highest discount at 19.12% and the lowest profit margin at 64.60%, while Enterprise-Pro has the lowest discount at 4.35% and the highest profit margin at 72.32%.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs24 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\fs24\insrsid9183818\charrsid9183818 Management Implication:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\fs24\insrsid9183818\charrsid9183818 \line Th
e analysis suggests that higher discount levels are associated with lower margins without producing a measurable retention benefit. Therefore, broad discounting could unnecessarily reduce profitability.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs24 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\fs24\insrsid9183818\charrsid9183818 Recommendation:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\fs24\insrsid9183818\charrsid9183818 \line 
